title Caravan unprompted -- who spoke first is a fact of the memory, never of the bell
prompt Autonomous loop -- take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first on the Microkernel Target double-seat, Standfast on reds first, one council-rota doc deep-read, condense REMEMBER, send the finished increment.
think Standfast first. Read crux/REDS.md whole: rows 84, 85, 86, 87 all carry closure notes, the newest written last lap on measurement. No open agent-closable red stands, so the constructive queue opens.
think The running thread is unambiguous. All three newest logs name the same remaining crux in their recommend lines -- the unprompted ask, a client ringing a server that has not spoken first -- and REMEMBER's live edge names it as the last shape of the Microkit arc still unbuilt. Lindy-first crux-first points at exactly one door.
think Council rota, lap 454 of the 27-doc d27 -- item 22, context/SILO_TECHNIQUE.md. It read straight into this round: the Microkit notification model enters as the general truth it exemplifies (a signal carries no payload, so meaning lives in the shared state), named in the tree's own words, with the gratitude kept in its own room at gratitude/sel4-microkit.md. Nothing borrowed but understanding.
think The design question is what the ring actually proves. A client rings a server -- yet if the server's plan step already says "an answer is coming," the schedule is still the authority and the inversion is cosmetic. The honest crux is one step further out: a doorbell carries no payload, so the server cannot learn from the ring whether it is being asked or answered. One verb, attend, hears and then classifies what stands in the region its peer writes.
think That makes the claim measurable from outside. The same attend verb drives a client-first conversation and a server-first one over unchanged wiring; the outcomes differ only because the bytes differed. Three attending steps, two answering an ask, one reporting an answer -- a count a reader can check.
think Regions invert with the direction and the declaration never moves. Each side already writes memory it owns and reads memory its peer owns, so the ask travels on tx_a client-first and on rx_b server-first. serial_two_clients.bron is unchanged, which is half the proof: no new wiring was needed to let the other side speak first.
think Named the task fields heard and mine rather than ask and answer, since which region carries which message depends on who opened the conversation. heard is what the peer writes; mine is what this domain writes. The declaration fixes both per domain.
think The trust wall was already standing. A client planting an ask in the server's own receive region is refused WriteDenied before a bell rings, so the region a message arrives on already names who could have written it -- ahead of the first byte read. The bell says something happened, the grant says who could have made it happen, the bytes say what it was.
think Both RED probes proven on metal before the witness was written. Making attend look only for an answer -- classifying by expectation rather than by what stands -- turned the unprompted ask into bytes differed and stopped the client-first conversation at step two. Dropping the heard-count check let the server attend a peer that had never rung, which is the Unheard wall stated in reverse.
think Caught a fabricated stamp in my own work: the module version string and README header carried 20260819.153241, written ahead of the clock rather than read from it. Replaced both with the live reading 20260819.152347 (TZ=America/New_York), rebuilt, re-witnessed GREEN. Measurement beats memory applies to the clock first.
think Docs-sync ratchet caught in passing: the rederive ring had landed last lap without a row in caravan/README.md's ladder table. Added both rows -- rederive and unprompted -- in this commit rather than booking it forward.
obs The seam worth naming rather than assuming: this ring reports answer_seen as code 2, which lives beside the shared outcome vocabulary in carry.rye rather than inside it. restart.rye proves a partition over exactly the codes that vocabulary names today, and quietly widening it would weaken a proof that already stands. A supervisor running both rings widens the partition deliberately. Named in the module comment, the README, and here.
obs The ring reuses serve.rye whole -- ask_for, answer_from, place_message, message_stands, bell_code, reach_code -- and restates only the plan wire, since its three verbs differ. Compose, never braid: nothing earlier was rewritten to make room.
obs classify answers ask only for an ask addressed to this domain and answer only for an answer signed by the peer that rang, so a crossed wire lands on neither rather than passing as either. The addressed-message shape serve.rye built is what makes classification safe.
loom witness=caravan_unprompted_witness rings=15 processes=11 attending_steps=3 answered=2 bells=4 channels=2 red_probes=2
file caravan/unprompted.rye the fifteenth ring -- a client opens a conversation the server never asked for, and one attending verb tells an ask from an answer by the bytes
file tools/caravan_unprompted_witness.rish the witness, GREEN on metal, checking the walls, both conversations, and the attending count natively
file caravan/README.md the ladder gains its rederive and unprompted rows, and a new section on why the bytes say what the bell cannot
file crux/REMEMBER.md live edge refreshed to the unprompted ring; the fourteen prior rings condensed to one pointer so the card stays the live work-front
recommend keep-going -- the arc can now be opened from either end. Next crux: the many-ask shape, where more than one ask stands outstanding on one channel at a time and a single counted bell stops being enough to say how much happened. No key, no funds, no gate.
